
🧑‍💻 Developer Workflow with Explanations
✅ 1. One-Time Setup Command
bash
Copy
Edit
./setup-formatting.sh
🔍 What it does:
Sets up a virtual environment (venv/)

Installs Python formatting/linting tools: black, flake8, isort, pylint, etc.

Creates all the necessary configuration files

Configures Git pre-commit hooks (so your code is checked before every commit)

Sets up VS Code settings to auto-format code on save

Creates format.sh, check.sh, and a Makefile with helpful commands

💡 You only run this once when you clone or start a new project.

🎨 2. Format All Python Files
bash
Copy
Edit
./format.sh
# OR
make format
🔍 What it does:
Runs black . → formats all .py files to follow consistent spacing, quotes, and style.

Runs isort . → automatically sorts your imports into standard sections (stdlib, third-party, project).

💡 Use this before pushing code to clean up formatting.

🔍 3. Check Code Without Modifying
bash
Copy
Edit
./check.sh
# OR
make check
🔍 What it does:
black --check . → checks if code is formatted correctly (no changes made).

isort --check-only . → checks if imports are ordered correctly.

flake8 . → lints the code for common issues and stylistic problems.

💡 Use this in CI/CD or before committing to see if anything fails.

🧪 4. Run Linting Only
bash
Copy
Edit
make lint
🔍 What it does:
Runs only the flake8 linter to check your code for errors, unused variables, bad practices, etc.

💡 Useful for focusing on code quality rather than formatting.

🔁 5. Automatically Run Checks Before Git Commit
No command needed — this works automatically once setup is done.

🔍 What happens:
When you run:

bash
Copy
Edit
git add .
git commit -m "Some message"
Git will automatically run:

black, isort, flake8, and others via pre-commit

It will block the commit if something fails

💡 Prevents bad or unformatted code from reaching your main branch.

📦 6. Install/Update Formatting Tools Again
bash
Copy
Edit
make install
🔍 What it does:
Installs everything listed in .formatting-requirements.txt

Reinstalls the pre-commit hooks

💡 Use this if you clone the repo or if tools/configs are updated.

